{"challenges":[{"attack":"Test the excluded input x=2, where cancellation might improperly enlarge the domain.","claim_id":"original-domain","outcome":"survives","result":"At x=2 the denominator x-2 is 0, so the original quotient is undefined; all other real x have nonzero denominator.","tool_ids":[]},{"attack":"Check that cancellation is performed only when the common factor is nonzero.","claim_id":"simplified-form","outcome":"survives","result":"The factorization x^2-4=(x-2)(x+2) is verified by expansion. On the stated domain x≠2, x-2 is nonzero and cancellation yields x+2.","tool_ids":[]},{"attack":"Test whether the simplified formula has acquired a value at an input excluded by the original expression.","claim_id":"not-global-equivalence","outcome":"survives","result":"At x=2, x+2=4 while the original quotient is undefined. Thus they agree on the original domain but are not equivalent as expressions at every real input.","tool_ids":[]}],"checks":[{"basis_reasoning":"The question contains the exact premise: “For real x where the expression is defined,”. This supports restricting the requested simplification to the original expression’s real domain.","basis_verdict":"applicable","checked_step_ids":[],"claim_id":"defined-scope","reasoning":"The stated scope accurately reflects the question’s domain qualification.","verdict":"supported"},{"basis_reasoning":"The derivation in step-domain states the required nonzero-denominator condition and solves x-2≠0 as x≠2.","basis_verdict":"applicable","checked_step_ids":["step-domain"],"claim_id":"original-domain","reasoning":"For real x, the quotient is defined exactly when x-2 is nonzero, hence exactly on ℝ\\{2}.","verdict":"supported"},{"basis_reasoning":"Steps step-factor and step-cancel explicitly factor the numerator and cancel only under the already established condition x≠2.","basis_verdict":"applicable","checked_step_ids":["step-domain","step-factor","step-cancel"],"claim_id":"simplified-form","reasoning":"Expanding (x-2)(x+2) gives x²-4. Since x-2≠0 on the domain, division by that factor is valid, proving the equality for every real x≠2.","verdict":"supported"},{"basis_reasoning":"Step-nonequivalence evaluates the domain distinction at x=2 using the established denominator restriction and the direct value 2+2=4.","basis_verdict":"applicable","checked_step_ids":["step-domain","step-nonequivalence"],"claim_id":"not-global-equivalence","reasoning":"The original quotient is undefined at x=2, whereas x+2 is defined there and equals 4. Therefore the cancellation result is not an everywhere-real equivalence.","verdict":"supported"}],"missing_evidence":[],"recommended_action":"finish","tool_requests":[]}
